#==============================================================================================
#
#   canvas - The editor's document
#
#   DESCRIPTION:
#       The Canvas is the one Luce-facing object an editor drives: the layer stack, the
#       brush, selections, adjustments, previews, history, files and drawing. Its methods
#       live in the fragment of their concern, under `extend Canvas:`, and delegate to the
#       engine (Document, Composer, luce-painting's Painter, Adjuster, Filters,
#       Transformer, luce-vector). This fragment holds its fields, the per-layer Record
#       and its lifecycle. Getters that read a layer take its stack index; setters take
#       its id. Fragments, in ORDER, by concern:
#
#       layers, layer_masks, layer_clip         the stack, masks, clipping, styles, layers copied
#       painting, adjusting, sizing             strokes and fills, adjustments, canvas and image size
#       drawing, bands, histograms              the view, 8-bit bands for export and copy, Levels
#       files, opening*, package_*, raw_tiles   pictures, Photoshop files and .l2d documents
#       clipboard, mask_fill                    copy and paste; masks made a cell at a time
#       retouch*, liquifying, mesh_warping      retouching tools, Liquify, Warp and Puppet Warp
#       transforming, *_previews                transforms and live previews
#       type_layers, shape_layers, vector_*,    text, shapes and vector layers (drawn by
#       graph_layers                            luce-vector)
#       ranging, selecting, selection_preview,  selections: Color Range, shapes, the wand,
#       refining, magnetic, masking, quick_mask Transform Selection, Magnetic Lasso, Select
#                                               and Mask, Quick Mask
#       channels, package_channels              alpha channels
#       history, memory, support, warp_trace    undo, the memory governor, binding, tracing
#       *_tests, test_support                   the canvas tests, by concern
#
#==============================================================================================

## The editor's document as one Luce-owned object: layers with names,
## visibility and opacity, pixels loaded from files, a flattened save, and
## drawing at any zoom and offset onto a render target. GPU storage is bound to
## the device of the first target it is drawn on; until then layers and their
## pictures wait on the CPU, so a document can be built before it is shown.
